## Overview

This configuration provisions the OCI networking required to enable Oracle Data Guard
between two Availability Domains (AZs) **within the same OCI region**.

| Resource | What it creates |
|---|---|
| `oci_core_local_peering_gateway.primary_lpg` | LPG on the Primary VM Cluster VCN — immediately peered |
| `oci_core_local_peering_gateway.standby_lpg` | LPG on the Standby VM Cluster VCN |
| `oci_core_network_security_group_security_rule` × 2 | TCP 1521 ingress rules on each NSG{% if add_ssh %} + SSH (port 22) rules{% endif %} |
{% if manage_route_table %}
| `oci_core_default_route_table.primary_rt` | Route to standby subnet via Primary LPG |
| `oci_core_default_route_table.standby_rt` | Route to primary subnet via Standby LPG |
{% else %}
| Route tables | ⚠ Not managed — add DG route rules manually after apply |
{% endif %}

The two LPGs are **immediately peered** at creation. This is a same-region
operation — no DRG or Remote Peering Connection is needed.

{% if manage_route_table %}
> **Route table mode: Terraform-managed.**
> `oci_core_default_route_table` resources are included. You must
> `terraform import` both route tables before the first `apply` — see
> *Route Table Management* below.
{% else %}
> **Route table mode: Manual.**
> `oci_core_default_route_table` is **not** generated. After `terraform apply`,
> add the DG route rule to each VM Cluster VCN manually — see
> *Route Table Management* below.
{% endif %}

