## Switching Between Route Table Modes

{% if manage_route_table %}
### Switching from Manual → Terraform-managed

Import both route tables into state before the first apply:

```bash
terraform import \
  oci_core_default_route_table.primary_rt \
  {{ primary_route_table_id or '<primary-default-rt-ocid>' }}

terraform import \
  oci_core_default_route_table.standby_rt \
  {{ standby_route_table_id or '<standby-default-rt-ocid>' }}
```

Then follow the *Read → Update → Verify* steps in *Route Table Management*
before running `terraform apply`.

{% else %}
### Switching from Terraform-managed → Manual (your current mode)

If you previously applied in Terraform-managed mode (or ran `terraform import`),
the route table resources are still tracked in Terraform state even though the
`resource` block no longer exists in `main.tf`.

**Terraform will plan to destroy or modify the route tables** on the next apply
unless you remove them from state first.

Run this **before** your next `terraform apply`:

```bash
terraform state rm oci_core_default_route_table.primary_rt
terraform state rm oci_core_default_route_table.standby_rt
```

After removing from state, Terraform treats the route tables as unmanaged and
`terraform plan` will show no changes to them.

> **Verify with `terraform plan`** after running `state rm`. The output should
> contain no reference to `oci_core_default_route_table`. If it still appears,
> check: `terraform state list | grep route_table`

{% endif %}

